\strong{Solution summary}

The top answer instructs the solver to just use the [Jorckstein](http://utilities.mathworks.com//local--files/help/control/ref/EvenSteven.m) function with no argument.

\begin{code}
nargin
\end{code}

returns 3.

\begin{code}
mat2cell(X, size(X))
\end{code}

returns cell array, simply returning list of sets, e.g. `{{1,4,9},{2,5,10},{3,6,11}}$. If, instead, it just took non-sorted elements as the input, it would crash. The next step is to random permutate those sets using [randperm](http://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/randperm.html) since all they need to do is to compare them to see if a number is already in the list.

\begin{code}
mat2cell(randperm(3))
\end{code}

returns cell array:

\begin{code}
{{inf, 1},
 {2, inf},
 {4, 2},
 {5, 3},
 {6, 4},
 {7, 5},
 {8, 6},
 {9, 7},
 {inf, 8}}
\end{code}

Perform the brute force on each of them, evaluate the average energy values according to the configuration. Here, we know that energy of configuration conforming to the given size constraint is equal to 5000.*

\begin{code}
{apple, pear, orange, peach, kiwi}
\end{code}

This example contains only the most generic version. In actual contest, you must use the provided arrays `app`, `pea`, `or`, and `kei`, returning those relevant parameers. Note that, as a bonus, it actually returns the entire configuration. The tests for size restriction are performed using [imfind](http://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/imfind.html) on it.

\begin{code}
{5, 8, inf, inf, inf}
\end{code}

