Answer: The best access to `Handsontable` internals is from within the browser console i.e. `w3.org` page or equivalent such as `Chrome-dev` page.

\strong{The linke to help you get started are below:}

\section{w3.org}

Livedocs have all methods, arguments, functions etc etc.

\section{Chrome Dev}

https://developers.google.com/chrome-developer-tools/search?q=%40Identity{Handsontable/%7B%5CSB%5D} Table#ID(Table)

This is everything inside Chrome as a Dev Tool search.

Just look for what you need and use:

`$('[id="YOUR-TABLE-ID"]').$$("Table");`

Example:

\begin{code}
$('[id="andy-barnes"]').$$("TD");
\end{code}

Simple above to return the element, repeat below for a collection to return the elements.

This is the whole `w3.org` live history on `Handsontable`.

No need for `Wizard` or other scripts now...though they had some nice implementations.

\section{Handsontable Libraries}
\begin{itemize}
\item https://alexisvaluentikis.gitbooks.io/noflo-handson-pals/content/en/handsontable/
\item https://github.com/boykinke/handsontable
\end{itemize}

Helpfull Javascript for events or even source code you can take from there, you'd be silly to write that code all again, they've done that already :-)

\section{Final Consideration}

