Answer: Here:

\begin{code}
print > $1
\end{code}

you print to a file handle $1. It can be of type `XML`, `STDOUT`, `SYS`, `USER`, `WEWORK` etc. It can even be of type `FUNC` if it prints the value fetched with the `&gw` macro variable (see also `#word` and `#fun`).

\begin{code}
print >> $1
\end{code}

does the same but in append mode. Or if you want a new file instead:

\begin{code}
sub sig {
     # select the correct log-related variables
     my @vars = [#'POSSCAR', "#'POSGAS'!, #'NEIS'!,  '#'POSGAS_END_PROCESS'!];
     # open the specified file handle
     create 'filepath', Undef, 1 << $#args, P = 3 << $#vars[0], A = 16 << $#vars[1], ste = 0, solv = 0;
     # the selected variable is the line position
     @start_pos = `POSGAS` ? ($&) : ($#args), '&NA',
     # simply print the final blocks into the resulting file
     pass &#words[0..($#args-1)], none, none, ''
}
\end{code}

\begin{itemize}
\item Note the `$VAR` appending `undef` before to set a default value
\item the blank line is optional and even prints a newline
\end{itemize}

