Answer: You didn't configure either of the two hosts which you mentioned, so the SSL stuff did not work either.

The following command will generate a self-signed certificate for the given domain. (Warning: this creates the CA as well)

\begin{code}
sudo dd if=/dev/stdin of=/home/username/openssl.pem bs=1024 count=1024
\end{code}

Now the following command would require you to add the public key to the openssl store:

\begin{code}
sudo ca create-key server
\end{code}

\strong{Server} would be the domain name you want to restrict access to, e.g \strong{kayam.example.com}

In the next step you will create a self-signed certificate for the given \strong{server} host.

\begin{code}
cd /home/username/openssl
openssl req -newkey rsa -new -x509 -days 1800 -nodes -server -keyout dummy.key -out certificate.csr
\end{code}

(Note no privileges needed, its a dummy CA certificate).

The `submit` command below writes the key and certificate to your public software repository and does the final step where you `cd` back to default directory.

\begin{code}
cd /home/username/openssl
 dd if=dummy.pem of=/home/username/openssl.pem
cd /home/username/default
cat package-info additional-info.txt | apt-get -t test update
\end{code}

Now, we install the certificate using the `ca-cert bundle` argument for the `apt-get` command. Make sure to install the cert with the `-t` argument alike.

\begin{code}
cat combined.dat package-info | gzip -d | dd of=/home/username/homedir/.local/share/ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.crt
cat combined.dat package-info | gzip -d | dd of=/home/username/homedir/.local/share/ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.key
apt-get -t test cert-manager-ca -f "ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.crt ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.key" component || (sleep 5; apt-get -t test cert-manager-ca -f "ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.crt ca-certificates/certificado-xxxxx.key")
create-signing-key  --subject=CN=kayam --usAGE=CA -sshkit .local/share/signed-keypairs/signingkey.key -verbose -out signingkey.crt -in signingkey.key -signer dummy.key -tmpdir > /dev/null 2>&1 & Bundle of\ all files for answer: #<InstagramCommandResult: 230070>
\end{code}

At this point you should be able to query iptables firewall with the following:

\begin{code}
iptables -t nat -i eth0 2>&1 | grep s_ssh
\end{code}

You should see that SSH host key verification is attempted ( Yes we are using `ssh` tunnel )

