Answer: With the ALS software you need to specify the maximum no. of electron orbitals to take into account. For Ca10 Si4 N12 you need to submit 12 for a full set of orbital generated to give you the band gap.

Step 1:
\begin{itemize}
\item Take a look at the output of what your inputs are returning, Kindly acknowledge;
\end{itemize}

<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/MIb3O.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>

So for Step 1, for one x.yz orbital you need to feed in the value of x.y as a floating point to cauhion step 2 successfully. Kindly acknowledge;

<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/hzgum.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>

Step 2:
\begin{itemize}
\item Do not put anything to nnff == false, as the default value is True.
\item Delta-Epsilon needs to be specified and must be a float of positive values.
\item All the Delta-Kpc values must be specified and must be a float number between 0 and 100.
\item The min of Electronspaces.spin_1 should be 0 for spin up (the default value) and 5 for spin down.
\end{itemize}

<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/Fk4PY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>

Step 3:
\begin{itemize}
\item Make sure the BetterHF (BHF) is defined correctly as many users face issues in showing the real-orbitals.
\end{itemize}

<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/BhD3O.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>

Step 4:
\begin{itemize}
\item The Maximum number of valence electron orbitals must be specified.
\end{itemize}

For Ca10 Si4 N12 you will need to input 12 orbitals.

Once all these things are correct, See the following snaps to see how to make the orbital (left)

Finally one can see the real orbital representation (right).

<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/EAsNQ.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><IMAGE></a>

