Answer: `whatis -s` tells you you the recognised variables (descriptions) for the output, and also the output format. (Also gives you some help on how to do the `whatis` on a command line).

`echo $IF” -s` gives its description (as a shell variable).
\begin{itemize}
\item `echo ${(IF} -s` gives the description (`echo` accidentally puts quotes around `IF`)
\item `echo $(IF -s` gives the description.
\end{itemize}

It would be worth it to check which variables are multi-line (so you can accounts for this when formatting) in this case, `IF` is a multi-line variable.
\begin{itemize}
\item `IF` : `POSCAR` – the POSCAR STRUCTURE of the molecule produced by POSCAR2MOL/POSCAR2MS.file.  Nuclear commnds are entered after separating commas (semicolons are not permitted in case IDs).
\item `${IF`, `}`: command substitution. The command substitution unfolds the quotes around the command, so you should try it without the quotes.
\end{itemize}

