Answer: `/[INST]/` and `_inst_` are your keys, they are referring to the file name.

On the other hand, `[_inst_]/CURRINFO/BAND GAP` is your operator, this tells the parser which element itself is expected to be parsed. In our case, it refers to the instance of

\begin{code}
POWX(=BAND GAP)(=FEATURE)(=2022 01 04)(=GABOII)(=1(Bliss))
\end{code}

All in all, to find the parsed band gap on your problem file, you need to look in

\begin{code}
filename.prm.pawx.yaml/_inst_/hash_run.txt/CADUS.pawx.GABOII(1)//BAND GAP
\end{code}

The hash_run.txt file represents each element in the problem in a json fashion, so usually you will see something like

\begin{code}
key: ["POWX(=BAND GAP)(=FuoriX)(=2022 01 04)(=GABOII)(=1(Bliss))"]
\end{code}

And you will need to loop over the element in order to find the parsed band gap one by one. Because the program is parsing the element sequentially, the band gap is calculated based on the whole POSCAR, in this case, the contents of `filename.prm.pawx.yaml` in order.

These direction keys are very important, not just to find your parsed value but many other things as well, if you're not familiar with path organisation in HPCat, I would suggest to take a look at this page:
