Answer: According to the user guide, one refers to the [band gap](https://chemistry.stackexchange.com/q/30221/4945).

\begin{blockquote}

\strong{What does 'band gap'}  of  a structure mean? Closed states of electron motion in solids occur when there is an electrostatic  charge present and it is in balance with the energy of this electronic states.

The balance between these states is called the\strong{ band gap}. To get an, idea how the states are broken into electrons filled and unfilled states  one can think of the energy of the band
  as a potential called the value of the potential.
\end{blockquote}

In the case of H2O/Br4/N4/O4 it is essentially the difference of the published potentials (which doesn't fit a saddle but a double minimum of the convex spectrum). This wouldn't therefore make much sense in view of the geometry being claimed on the figure.

The next, higher level, description Concept (https://openmm.net/concepts) states the following:

\begin{blockquote}

\strong{Electronic states}  consists of a series of electronic states obtained from constraining \strong{valence electrons} in such a way. \strong{Wave nature} is conserved by the current method, resulting in the wave function being uniquely determined by its energy and ionic potential, guaranteeing the reduced wavefunction is correctly represented in Quantum Theory.
\end{blockquote}

